---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyProse from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyProse.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro";
import CaseStudyCards from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCards.astro";
import CaseStudyCard from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCard.astro";

import heroImage from "../../assets/images/ww-flooring-outlet/home.png";
import shopImage from "../../assets/images/ww-flooring-outlet/shop.png";
import productImage from "../../assets/images/ww-flooring-outlet/product.png";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Building the W&W Flooring Outlet"
	pageTitle="W&W Flooring Outlet case study - Will Jackson"
	description="How I built a click-and-collect WooCommerce store on a from-scratch block theme for Woven & Woods' one-off rugs, remnants and end-of-line flooring."
	path="/work/ww-flooring-outlet/"
	lede="A click-and-collect shop for one-off rugs, roll ends and end-of-line flooring, giving customers the chance to pick up interesting, quality pieces from a designer flooring retailer at a friendlier price."
	facts={[
		{ label: "Client", value: "Woven & Woods, flooring showrooms in Twickenham and Richmond" },
		{ label: "Role", value: "Design, development and hosting" },
		{ label: "Stack", value: "WordPress block theme, WooCommerce, Square, CloudPanel" },
		{ label: "Year", value: "2026" },
	]}
	heroImage={heroImage}
	heroImageAlt="The W&W Flooring Outlet homepage"
	link="https://outlet.wovenandwoods.com"
>
	<CaseStudyProse heading="The brief">
		<p>
			Projects and showroom updates leave Woven & Woods with spare rolls of
			carpet, end-of-line batches of wood and stair runner remnants. They're
			the same quality as everything in the showrooms, but they were
			sitting in the warehouse.
		</p>
		<p>
			Woven & Woods is a mid-to-high-end designer retailer, so this
			couldn't feel like a clearance sale. The outlet needed to present
			each piece as a find worth having, be easy for showroom staff to
			keep stocked, and fit around how the showrooms already work.
		</p>
	</CaseStudyProse>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="A block theme built from scratch"
		image={shopImage}
		imageAlt="The outlet shop, with the find, buy and collect steps, category filters and product cards"
	>
		<p>
			The main Woven & Woods site was a child theme of Twenty Twenty-Two at
			the time, so I built the outlet's block theme from scratch instead.
			Every template and style rule, in theme.json and style.css, is my
			own, which meant full control over the design and WooCommerce
			templates without fighting a parent theme's defaults.
		</p>
		<p>
			It also means the site only loads the CSS it needs, brand colours,
			type and spacing are defined once for both the editor and the front
			end, and no parent theme update can change the site unexpectedly.
			The outlet became the testbed for the approach I then used on the
			new main Woven & Woods website.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="From basket to collection"
		image={productImage}
		imageAlt="An outlet product page for a one-off sisal rug, with price, stock and Add to basket button"
		reverse
	>
		<p>
			Every item is one of a kind and sold exactly as described. Customers
			can search the stock instantly with FiboSearch, and products also
			appear in Google Shopping.
		</p>
		<p>
			Checkout runs on Square, the same provider the showrooms already use
			for card payments and invoices. When someone buys, they're emailed
			an invitation to collect, straight away if the showroom is open or
			from the next morning, while a staff Google Group gets the order so
			someone can prepare it. Everything is collected from the Richmond
			showroom, which keeps postage out of the picture.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudyCards>
		<CaseStudyCard heading="Easy for staff to keep stocked">
			<p>
				The first batch of stock went in with a CSV import. Since then,
				the Richmond showroom manager photographs and lists new pieces as
				they become available, using a step-by-step guide I wrote in the
				company knowledge base and an in-person walkthrough.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>

		<CaseStudyCard heading="Hosting and deployment">
			<p>
				The store runs on a Hetzner VPS managed with CloudPanel, which I
				chose after trialling RunCloud and Ploi, with caching built in.
				The theme is developed locally and deployed with GitHub Actions,
				and I used Claude Code as a tool for some of the logic.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>
	</CaseStudyCards>
</CaseStudyLayout>
